!!id:"https://tson.io/2026/32/ltr8/http/http-api-1.tn"
!!meta:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/meta.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/32/m/core.tn"
@doc:"""
  The vocabulary for describing an HTTP API in an ordinary schema: the enums, and the base record
  every operation composes.

  A separate schema only because imports now allow it (UPSTREAM.md #11). Before that fix a service
  could reference types from exactly one other schema, so an API description had to carry its own
  vocabulary, its own domain types and its own errors in one file. Splitting them is what a schema
  library looks like, and it is what the fix bought.

  Everything here would move a layer up if a meta-schema could declare an `operation` constructor;
  see sketch/README.md for what that changes.
  """
{
  http_method => !enum [GET POST PUT PATCH DELETE HEAD OPTIONS]

  @doc:"Refined, so 42 is not a status."
  status_code => !integer ^ { min: 100  max: 599 }

  @doc:"""
    One response: a status and a body. A template, so the shape is declared once and every response
    in every service is an application of it rather than another record that has to match by hand.

    S is a VALUE parameter filling a FIXED field -- `status: status_code = S` -- which is what makes
    this work at all. Without value parameters a status would have to be fixed per declaration, and
    each response would be a record again.

    An application cannot appear directly inside a choice (UPSTREAM.md #13), so an operation names
    its responses as entries first and chooses over the names. Each is still one line.
    """
  response => <T, S> { status: status_code = S  body: T }

  @doc:"""
    A page of results. The envelope pattern OpenAPI hand-rolls with allOf or repeats per endpoint,
    written once and applied -- `page<order>` is a real instantiation with structural identity
    (§8.2), deduped schema-wide.
    """
  page => <T> { items: [T]  next: uri?  total: int32 }

  @doc:"""
    Every operation composes this, so an operation is found in resolved output by its supertype
    rather than by a naming convention, and the resolver checks it has a method and a path.

    Composition narrows: an operation inherits `method` and re-declares it FIXED to its own verb.
    What this cannot state is that an operation has a response -- each operation's choice has
    different variants, so the base has nothing to name.
    """
  operation => { method: http_method  path: text }
}
